 Nifty Trading Plan for October 26, 2026 
 Nifty 50 needs to defend 22,372 support level
 Break below 22,372 could increase likelihood of further decline towards 22,180
 If index manages to hold this support, it could rebound towards 22,800-23,000
 Did our AI summary help? 
 Market Mastery
After severe selling pressure and broad-based weakness, the market is expected to consolidate in the coming sessions, while elevated oil prices may keep sentiment cautious. Technically, the Nifty 50 needs to defend the 22,372 support level, which coincides with Friday’s low and the 200-week EMA. A break below this level could increase the likelihood of a further decline towards the recent swing low of 22,180. However, if the index manages to hold this support, it could rebound towards 22,800-23,000. Meanwhile, further correction in the Bank Nifty could put the 53,800-53,700 support zone at risk. A break below this zone could intensify selling pressure. On the upside, any rebound may face resistance in the 54,500-55,000 range, followed by resistance at 55,300, according to experts.
On October 23, the Nifty 50 fell 88 points, or 0.39 percent, to close at 22,600, while the Bank Nifty declined 638 points, or 1.16 percent, to 54,287. Market breadth remained firmly in favour of the bears, with 2,863 stocks declining against 434 advancing stocks on the NSE.
 Osho Krishan, Chief Manager - Technical & Derivative Research at Angel One 
The broader market witnessed widespread weakness, with the mid-cap and small-cap indices registering significant declines, further dampening the overall market sentiment. From a technical perspective, the Nifty50 index failed to sustain above its 20-DEMA after struggling around for a couple of sessions. The index subsequently extended its decline towards the recent lows. Moreover, the RSI has delivered a negative crossover, signaling weakening momentum and warranting a cautious approach in the current week.
On the levels, 22,500 remains a crucial juncture, a decisive breach below this level could accelerate the decline towards 22,200 on intermediate basis, with the psychological mark of 22,000 potentially coming into focus thereafter. On the upside, the 20-DEMA, placed around 22,840, is likely to act as an immediate resistance and a sustained move above this hurdle would be essential to revive positive momentum and pave the way for a potential retest of 23,000, followed by 23,150 in the near term.
At present, the near-term bias remains tilted towards the downside, although the broader directional trend lacks definitive confirmation.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty Futures near 22,300 with a stop-loss of 22,000 and book profits near 22,800-23,000.
 Jatin Gedia, Technical Research Analyst, VP, Derivatives & Technical Research at Teji Mandi 
Nifty continues to consolidate in the range 22,350 - 23,200 for the fourth week holding above the crucial support zone of 22,200 - 22,100. While the Nifty consolidates, selling pressure has shifted towards the Mid, Small and Microcap universe which were down 5.6% , 7.27%, and 7.42% respectively last week.
On the Monthly charts, the Nifty is in the process of forming a Doji candlestick pattern. Though the month is not over yet, we anticipate this, taking into consideration the rangebound price action along with the divergent momentum setup on the daily and weekly time frame charts. This is important because a Doji pattern forming after a multi-month decline suggests that selling pressure is exhausting and also indicates the existing downtrend is about to reverse.
With this backdrop, we expect the Nifty to consolidate within 22,390 (200-week exponential moving average) - 23,060 (20-week simple moving average). As we move towards the October series derivatives expiry, the maximum Call open interest is placed at 23,000 strike while the maximum Put open interest is placed at 22,500 strike. Significant addition in open interest was witnessed at the 22,500 Call and Put strikes, suggesting that a 22,500 short straddle is being deployed. The 22,500 straddle is trading at Rs 304 which translates into a range of Rs 22,200 - 22,800.
While the yields have cooled off from 5.36 percent to 4.97 percent, Brent crude oil futures above $110 is a near term risk. If the Nifty sustains below 22,350 for a couple of sessions, it could drift swiftly towards 22,000 - 21,740 which is the next major support.
 Strategy : Buy Nifty October Futures above 22,670, with a stop-loss of 22,500, and target of 22,960.
 Vaishnavi Jagtap, Senior Research Analyst – Technical at Axis Direct 
